  "knowledgePattern": "Turn two distinct intersections into a strict discriminant bound",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Substitute the line into the circle",
      "body": "<p>Replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in the circle equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. Collect the resulting expression as a quadratic in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "A common point must satisfy both equations.",
      "pitfall": "Keep brackets around the whole substituted expression before squaring.",
      "pause": "Write the quadratic in x.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-simultaneous-equations",
          "reminder": "A common point must satisfy both equations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Require two crossings",
      "body": "<p>Use the discriminant condition for two different real roots. Then count the integer values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> that satisfy it.</p>",
      "recap": "A positive discriminant gives two distinct real roots.",
      "pitfall": "Equality gives a tangent, not two distinct points.",
      "pause": "Solve the strict inequality and count its integer values.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l03",
          "reminder": "A positive discriminant gives two distinct real roots."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> into the circle: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Each real root gives one intersection point.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> Two distinct intersections require <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>c</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>400</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> The integer values are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>, giving 19 values. Answer A. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> there is a tangent rather than two distinct points, so neither endpoint is counted.</p>"
